.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt EQN 7
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm eqn
.Nd equations in manual pages
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
language describes mathematical notation inside a manual page written in
.Xr mdoc 7
or
.Xr man 7 .
It describes how an equation is laid out, not what it means.
Like
.Xr tbl 7 ,
it began as a preprocessor that turned its input into
.Xr roff 7
requests;
.Xr oxdoc 1
reads it directly, as part of the page.
.Pp
An equation starts with a
.Ic \&EQ
line and ends with an
.Ic \&EN
line; the lines between them are the equation.
It takes its place in the text around it, as a word would:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
The sum
\&.EQ
sum from i=1 to n i sup 2
\&.EN
grows as the cube of n.
.Ed
.Pp
An equation can also be written inside a text line, between the
delimiters the
.Cm delim
statement sets.
.Sh WORDS
An equation is a sequence of words separated by white space: spaces, tabs,
line ends, and the characters
.Ql ^
and
.Ql ~ ,
which are only separators here.
Braces
.Pq Ql {\& No and Ql }\&
are words by themselves, and a string in double quotes is one word, spaces
and all; inside quotes,
.Ql \e\(dq
stands for a quote.
Anything else runs up to the next separator, brace or quote, so
.Ql x+1
is one word.
.Pp
A word that is not a keyword is set as text.
An unquoted word is split where its characters change kind: letters, digits
(with a decimal point), and anything else.
Letters are set in italic, the rest in roman, and the pieces are spaced as
mathematics usually is: a space around an operator such as
.Ql + ,
none before a comma or a closing parenthesis, none after an opening one.
.Pp
Some words are set as a symbol instead:
.Bl -column -offset indent "partial" "∂ partial derivative" "partial" "∂ partial derivative"
.It Cm alpha Ta \(*a Ta Cm DELTA Ta \(*D
.It Cm beta Ta \(*b Ta Cm GAMMA Ta \(*G
.It Cm gamma Ta \(*g Ta Cm LAMBDA Ta \(*L
.It Cm delta Ta \(*d Ta Cm OMEGA Ta \(*W
.It Cm epsilon Ta \(*e Ta Cm PHI Ta \(*F
.It Cm zeta Ta \(*z Ta Cm PI Ta \(*P
.It Cm eta Ta \(*y Ta Cm PSI Ta \(*Q
.It Cm theta Ta \(*h Ta Cm SIGMA Ta \(*S
.It Cm iota Ta \(*i Ta Cm THETA Ta \(*H
.It Cm kappa Ta \(*k Ta Cm UPSILON Ta \(*U
.It Cm lambda Ta \(*l Ta Cm XI Ta \(*C
.It Cm mu Ta \(*m Ta Cm sum Ta \[sum]
.It Cm nu Ta \(*n Ta Cm prod Ta \[product]
.It Cm xi Ta \(*c Ta Cm int Ta \[integral]
.It Cm omicron Ta \(*o Ta Cm union Ta \(cu
.It Cm pi Ta \(*p Ta Cm inter Ta \(ca
.It Cm rho Ta \(*r Ta Cm grad , del Ta \(gr
.It Cm sigma Ta \(*s Ta Cm partial Ta \(pd
.It Cm tau Ta \(*t Ta Cm inf Ta \(if
.It Cm upsilon Ta \(*u Ta Cm times Ta \(mu
.It Cm phi Ta \(*f Ta Cm cdot Ta \(pc
.It Cm chi Ta \(*x Ta Cm approx Ta \(~~
.It Cm psi Ta \(*q Ta Cm prime Ta \(fm
.It Cm omega Ta \(*w Ta Cm half Ta \(12
.El
.Pp
The operators
.Ql <= ,
.Ql >= ,
.Ql != ,
.Ql == ,
.Ql +- ,
.Ql -> ,
.Ql <- ,
.Ql <<
and
.Ql >> ,
as words of their own, are set as the symbols
.Sq \(<= ,
.Sq \(>= ,
.Sq \(!= ,
.Sq \(== ,
.Sq \(+- ,
.Sq \(-> ,
.Sq \(<- ,
.Sq \(<<
and
.Sq \(>> ;
.Cm nothing
is an empty word.
The names of common functions,
.Cm sin , cos , tan , sinh , cosh , tanh , coth , sec , csc , atan , arc ,
.Cm exp , log , ln , lim , max , min , det , \&Re , Im ,
and the words
.Cm and , if
and
.Cm for ,
are set in roman.
Escape sequences, such as the special characters of
.Xr roff 7 ,
work in any word.
.Sh BOXES
An equation is built of boxes.
A word is a box, and braces make a sequence of boxes into one.
The keywords combine boxes:
.Bl -tag -width "matrix { lcol { ... } ... }"
.It Ar a Cm over Ar b
A fraction,
.Ar a
over
.Ar b .
.It Ar a Cm sup Ar b
.Ar b
as a superscript of
.Ar a .
.It Ar a Cm sub Ar b
.Ar b
as a subscript of
.Ar a .
.Ar a Cm sub Ar b Cm sup Ar c
sets both on
.Ar a .
.It Ar a Cm from Ar b
.Ar b
under
.Ar a ,
as the limits of a sum or an integral are set.
.It Ar a Cm to Ar b
.Ar b
over
.Ar a .
.Ar a Cm from Ar b Cm to Ar c
sets both on
.Ar a .
.It Cm sqrt Ar a
The square root of
.Ar a .
.It Cm left Ar c Ar ... Cm right Ar d
The boxes between, enclosed in the delimiters
.Ar c
and
.Ar d ,
which may be empty
.Pq Ql \(dq\(dq .
Without
.Cm right ,
the equation ends with a closing parenthesis.
.It Cm pile Brq Ar a Cm above Ar b Cm above Ar ...
The boxes one above the other, centred;
.Cm lpile
and
.Cm rpile
align them left and right,
.Cm cpile
centres them.
.It Cm matrix Brq Cm lcol Brq Ar ... Cm ccol Brq Ar ... Cm rcol Brq Ar ...
A matrix of columns, each a pile of the same number of boxes, aligned left,
centred or right.
.It Ar a Cm bar , Cm under
A line over or under
.Ar a .
.It Ar a Cm dot , Cm dotdot , Cm hat , Cm tilde , Cm vec , Cm dyad
A dot, two dots, a circumflex, a tilde, an arrow or a double arrow over
.Ar a .
.It Cm roman , Cm italic , Cm bold , Cm fat Ar a
.Ar a
in that font;
.Cm fat
is bold.
Words inside
.Ar a
are not split.
.It Cm size Ar n Ar a
.Ar a
in point size
.Ar n ;
the size is ignored.
.El
.Pp
Marks bind tightest: a mark belongs to the box just before it.
Then come the fonts,
.Cm size
and
.Cm sqrt ,
each taking the one box after it.
.Cm sub
and
.Cm sup
come next and group to the right, so
.Ql x sup a sup b
is
.Ql x sup { a sup b } ;
.Cm over
is looser and groups to the left.
.Cm from
and
.Cm to
group to the right, and apply to what the other operations have made of the
boxes before them.
Braces override all of this.
.Sh STATEMENTS
These words change how the rest of the page's equations are read; they set
no box.
.Bl -tag -width "define name 'value'"
.It Cm define Ar name Ar value
From here on, the word
.Ar name
stands for
.Ar value .
The first character of
.Ar value
is its delimiter: it runs up to the next occurrence of that character, so
.Ql define half \&'1 over 2\&'
defines
.Ql half .
A definition may use others; they are expanded when it is used.
.Cm ndefine
is the same;
.Cm tdefine
is read and discarded.
.It Cm undef Ar name
Forgets a definition.
.It Cm delim Ar xy
Makes
.Ar x
and
.Ar y
the delimiters of equations inside text lines.
.Ql delim off
turns them off, and
.Ql delim on
back on.
.It Cm gfont Ar font , Cm gsize Ar size
Set the default font and size; they are read and ignored.
.It Cm back , fwd , up , down Ar n
Move the next box; they are read and ignored.
.It Cm mark , lineup
Align equations with each other; they are ignored.
.El
.Sh IN-LINE EQUATIONS
Once
.Cm delim
has set delimiters, an equation can be written in the middle of a text or
macro line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.EQ
delim $$
\&.EN
The area is $pi r sup 2$.
.Ed
.Pp
The line is cut around the equation.
The text before it stays as it was; the equation follows it, without a
space unless the line has one there; and the rest of the line is text,
even after a macro's arguments, joined to the equation the same way.
.Sh OUTPUT
On a terminal, an equation is set on one line, with parentheses where its
structure would otherwise be lost:
.Bl -column -offset indent "x sub i sup 2" "(a + b)/(c + d)"
.It Sy Input Ta Sy Output
.It Li a+b over c+d Ta a + b/(c + d)
.It Li {a+b} over {c+d} Ta (a + b)/(c + d)
.It Li x sub i sup 2 Ta x_i^2
.It Li sqrt {x+1} Ta \(sr(x + 1)
.It Li x bar Ta x\(rn
.El
.Pp
In HTML, an equation is MathML.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The quadratic formula,
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.EQ
x = {-b +- sqrt {b sup 2 - 4ac}} over 2a
\&.EN
.Ed
.Pp
is shown as
.EQ
x = {-b +- sqrt {b sup 2 - 4ac}} over 2a
.EN
.Pp
A sum with limits, using a definition:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.EQ
define sq 'i sup 2'
sum from i=1 to n sq = {n(n+1)(2n+1)} over 6
\&.EN
.Ed
.Pp
is shown as
.EQ
define sq 'i sup 2'
sum from i=1 to n sq = {n(n+1)(2n+1)} over 6
.EN
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
In lint mode,
.Xr oxdoc 1
reports problems in equations at the levels
.Xr mandoc 1
uses: a closing brace,
.Cm right
or
.Cm above
with nothing open for it to close, an operation missing its first box, a
statement missing its argument, an unterminated quoted word or definition,
and a definition that uses itself.
